This is an enclosure substitution, not an exact-output optimization.

All constructors and primitive operations enclose the exact real operation on
input intervals. For b>0, Python integer floor and -((-a)//b) give the lower
and upper integral bounds for a/b including negative a. Addition of intervals
is exact at scale S. Rational scalar addition separately rounds both endpoints
outwards. Negation reverses endpoints. Products use the four corner extrema;
scalar multiplication uses its sign. Division requires the stored divisor
lower endpoint strictly positive and encloses all four endpoint quotients,
so it is sound for either sign of the numerator. Odd powers are monotone;
even powers use zero if crossed and the extreme endpoint powers otherwise.
S**(n-1) is the exact positive rescaling factor. The zero power returns one.

The Bernstein transform is sound: signed translation weights select the
appropriate input endpoint for each requested direction. Rounding each
weighted summand outward and adding preserves that direction. All degree-
elevation weights are nonnegative exact binomial ratios, so the second
outward rounding preserves a lower/upper bound for every exact transformed
coefficient. The min/max Bernstein inequalities therefore remain valid.
The optimized joint cubic code adds exact scalar affine translations to
these coefficient bounds; widening their interval-only base is conservative.

